1. Based on our first implementation, which of the following apply?
    A. We are coding to concrete implementations, not interfaces.
        - WeatherData depends on the concrete display classes. If those classes change, WeatherData might break
        - Violates Principle: Program to an interface, not an implementation.
    B. For every new display element, we need to alter code.
        - The subject (WeatherData) must be modified every time a new display is added. Adding a new display
          requires modifying existing, stable code (WeatherData), instead of extending behavior through polymorphism.
        - Violates Principle: Open for extension, closed for modification (Open/Closed Principle)
    C. We have no way to add display elements at runtime.
        - Displays are wired in as explicit calls of concrete implementations so we can't
          dynamically subscribe/unsubscribe at runtime.
        - Violates Principle: Favor loose coupling between objects that interact.
    E. We haven't encapsulated what changes.
        - The number and type of displays change and that variability is baked into WeatherData
        - Violates Principle: Identify what varies and encapsulate it from what stays the same.